If I did my math right, it looks like the expected number of charges
to write a book is (for non wiz):

(expected # of charges for ONE try) / (.32). I did some weird math
involving summing an arithmetico-geometric series to come up with that
number that doesn't really get copied into a usenet forum very well,
but does someone want to check me on that number?

Anyway, assuming that math is right, we get 1 try for a spellbook, say
drain life, which is 20 charges, is 15. Then 15 / .32 = 46.875. So we
expect to write a book as non wiz it will take about 47 charges.

Now the expected number of charges for a marker is about 65 (generated
30 - 99). Subtract the expected 12 charges to make a charing scroll
and we get 53, then add the 50 for charging it and we get 103.
Approximately.

With 103 charges available, multiply by .32 and we get the breakeven
point for wishing for a fresh marker vs a spellbook to be almost
exactly 33. Its a little worse than that because sometimes we lose a
few charges on the recharges setting an empty marker to 3.

So basically, if my math is right, if you really want a specific spell
(jumping, drain life, magic missile) it makes sense to wish for a
marker and try to write the book. However, if you instead want
something higher level (magic mapping, detect treasure, finger of
death, polymorph), you should just wish for the book.

Of course wizards should always wish for a marker unless the spell is
level 7 (where they might not get enough charges in the marker at
all). And since they get finger of death by crowning, that really only
means cancellation - and I can't really think of a situation where
that's the most productive use of a wish anyway ;)

Thoughts?

